      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Statement of Cash Flows: Reporting Gains on Sale of Equipment</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">In operating activities as a deduction from income.</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Explanation</h3><p style="margin:0">When using the indirect method for preparing a statement of cash flows, we start with net income and adjust it to arrive at cash flows from operating activities. Since a gain on sale of equipment is already included in the net income but does not represent an operating cash inflow, we must remove it from operating activities.</p><p style="margin:0">The gain represents the excess of the cash received over the book value of the equipment. Since the entire cash received from the sale is reported in the investing section, we must deduct the gain from net income in the operating section to avoid double-counting.</p></div><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Example</h3><p style="margin:0">Assume a company sells equipment with a book value of $8,000 for $10,000 cash, resulting in a $2,000 gain.</p></div><div style="background-color:#f5f9fc; border-left:5px solid #207bb5; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Statement of Cash Flows Presentation (Indirect Method)</h3><p style="color:#01506e; font-weight:600; margin:14px 0 6px 0">Statement of Cash Flows (Partial)</p><table class="mx-table" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><tbody><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Cash flows from operating activities:</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px"></td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Net income</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$XXXX</td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Adjustments to reconcile net income to net cash provided by operating activities:</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px"></td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Gain on sale of equipment</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">($2,000)</td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Cash flows from investing activities:</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px"></td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Proceeds from sale of equipment</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$10,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="background-color:white; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Analysis of Answer Options</h3><table cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Option</th><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Analysis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">In investment activities as a cash outflow</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect. The gain is not a cash outflow; it&#x27;s a non-cash item that needs to be removed from the operating section.</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">In operating activities as an addition to income</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect. The gain is already included in net income, so it needs to be deducted, not added.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><p style="margin:0">When using the indirect method for a statement of cash flows:</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">A gain on sale of equipment is deducted from net income in the operating activities section</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The entire cash proceeds from the sale are reported in the investing activities section</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">This approach prevents double-counting of the gain portion of the proceeds</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Gains and losses on sales of non-current assets are always adjustments to net income in the operating section when using the indirect method</li></ul></div></div></div>`,
